+## 1. Finding (filer's evidence, 2026-10-28)
+
+The public HTTP `/health` route on both the MCP tower (`supremeai-mcp-tower.onrender.com/health`) and the core API served a **frozen snapshot** while the MCP `health_full_sweep` engine remained fully live. Fifteen hourly WebFetch reads from 09:57 to 23:57 (+08) returned a tower `/health` timestamp of exactly `2026-10-24T11:41:20.752Z` and an api `/health` timestamp of exactly `2026-10-24T11:40:55.206300+00:00`, ms-identical on every read. Earlier morning reads showed a *different* frozen value (`21:57:38.375Z`) persisting across two cycles — a refresh-never-happens pattern, not a one-off stale window.
+
+In the same window, the same-session MCP `health_full_sweep` returned live results (`checkedAt 2026-10-28T16:58:51Z`, 2890 ms, `incidents: []`), proving the sweep engine and its Redis plane were healthy while the HTTP route never republished. The two planes had diverged: consumers scraping the public HTTP health endpoint were reading a 4-day-old verdict of system state, while the actual checker plane was current.
+
+Operational impact: any watchdog, dashboard, or external monitor keyed on the HTTP `/health` body (rather than on MCP sweep output) was blind for up to four days. This is the monitoring-equivalent of the verdict-blackout family (#2100): the data plane works, the advertisement plane freezes.
+
+## 2. Live resolution receipt (solver, 2026-10-28 ~18:06Z)
+
+During claim processing, the render-mcp redeploy that also unlocked the queue-r2 plane (ladder rung `limit=200` accepted at ~18:05Z, after ~17h of `-32602 expected <=50`; see #2211/#2212 for the deploy-failure family and restart bracket) also **resolved the frozen route**:
+
+| Read | Endpoint | Timestamp served | Delta |
+|------|----------|------------------|-------|
+| 1 | tower `/health` | `2026-10-28T18:06:25.444Z` | live |
+| 2 | tower `/health` | `2026-10-28T18:06:49.090Z` | +23.6 s |
+| 3 | tower `/health` | `2026-10-28T18:06:52.175Z` | +3.1 s |
+| 4 | tower `/health` | `2026-10-28T18:06:55.347Z` | +3.2 s |
+
+Reads 2–4 were taken ~3 s apart and the timestamp **advances per request** — the defect signature (ms-identical reads across hours) is gone. MCP `health_full_sweep` at `18:06:28Z` (2530 ms, `incidents: []`) agrees with the HTTP plane again.
+
+Interpretation: the frozen snapshot was a build-time/boot-time artifact of the *previous* container build (consistent with #2212's restart evidence: session store and health cache died together 17:10–17:16Z). The new build republishes `/health` per request (or on a live cache TTL), which closes the freshness gap at the root.
+
+## 3. Guard proposal — freshness gate (fake-clock, unit + integration)
+
+The resolution is deploy-luck, not an invariant. The failure mode "HTTP route serves a snapshot while the sweep engine is live" had no detector: sentry passes (#1934 series) probe providers, and the plane watches (#2204 series) probe the bridge queue, but nothing compares HTTP `/health` freshness against MCP sweep freshness. Proposed guards, to be wired in a follow-up code WO (this WO is doc-only by scope):
+
+### 3.1 Unit tier — staleness guard function (fake clock)
+
+```python
+# scripts/ci/health_freshness_guard.py (proposal — NOT wired in this doc-only change)
+from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone
+
+MAX_STALENESS = timedelta(minutes=10)   # /health must never lag now by more than this
+
+def parse_ts(value: str) -> datetime:
+    """Parse ISO-8601 timestamps served by /health; fail-closed on junk."""
+    ts = datetime.fromisoformat(value.replace("Z", "+00:00"))
+    if ts.tzinfo is None:
+        raise ValueError("naive timestamp: fail-closed")
+    return ts
+
+def staleness_seconds(health_body: dict, now: datetime) -> float:
+    """Positive = seconds stale. Raises on missing/naive timestamp (fail-closed)."""
+    return (now - parse_ts(health_body["timestamp"])).total_seconds()
+
+def is_fresh(health_body: dict, now: datetime) -> bool:
+    return staleness_seconds(health_body, now) <= MAX_STALENESS.total_seconds()
+```
+
+Fake-clock unit cases (no network, deterministic):
+
+1. `now == ts` → fresh (0 s staleness).
+2. `now == ts + 9 min` → fresh (inside budget).
+3. `now == ts + 11 min` → STALE (exit 1).
+4. frozen snapshot: two bodies with byte-identical timestamps read at different fake `now`s → second read STALE; also flags the frozen pattern itself (identical-ts detector).
+5. missing `timestamp` key, naive timestamp, junk string → raise/fail-closed (never silently fresh).
+6. clock-skew guard: `ts > now + 5 min` (future timestamp) → fail-closed, catches clock lies both directions.
+
+### 3.2 Integration tier — dual-plane cross-check + loop watchdog
+
+- **Cross-check probe** (zero-credential, stdlib-only, per the #2076/#2082 probe discipline): fetch HTTP `/health`, fetch MCP `health_full_sweep` in the same session, assert `(sweep.checkedAt - http.timestamp)` stays within budget. Receipt from this cycle: pre-redeploy the gap was **4 days** (frozen route vs live sweep); post-redeploy it is **~3 s**. The gap metric is the detector.
+- **Frozen-read detector**: persist the previous `/health` timestamp; if the new read is byte-identical to the previous one, count a strike. Two consecutive identical reads at different wall-clock times = refresh-never-happens = fail-close. (This catches the exact filer signature: 15 ms-identical hourly reads.)
+- **Loop watchdog wiring**: the continuous loop's U3 step (render deployStatus vs HTTP aliveness) already walks both planes; add the freshness cross-check as a fourth signal so a frozen route is reported as `health-frozen` in cycle receipts, distinct from `deploy-failed` and `http-dead`.
+- **Wiring note**: pre-push/CI wiring follows the roster pattern used by `radar_index_freshness_guard` and `docs_link_integrity_guard` (roster entry + pre-push section, zero `.github/**` edits per #1643). The guard itself must be stdlib-only and offline per the #1760 honest-skip convention: tower unreachable → honest-skip, not failure.
